  "summary": "The Half Moon Bay City Council voted 3-2 on April 16, 2024 to move forward with a two-year pilot installing seven automated license plate readers, funded by a federal COPS grant. Staff will draft a Flock Safety contract for final approval, and the city manager said Half Moon Bay should consider adopting a surveillance use ordinance like Palo Alto's.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "As a pilot, I am for the program. I cannot say I want ALPRs in my town for the rest of the millennium. I can say it's worth a two-year pilot program",
      "paragraph_idx": 4
    },
    {
      "quote": "ALPR data has a 30-day retention period, does not record faces or any personal information and cannot be used to enforce another state's laws or immigration laws and is not stored by third-party databases",
      "paragraph_idx": 5
    },
    {
      "quote": "Vehicles do change hands. If a vehicle is reported to be in a crime, robbery, shooting, hit-and-run, and the vehicle within a month is sold or is being driven by a different person, the person driving the vehicle will be responsible. And that is my concern",
      "paragraph_idx": 7
    },
    {
      "quote": "Staff will draw up a contract with Flock Safety \u2014 an ALPR company used by other cities in San Mateo County \u2014 and return to the City Council for final approval",
      "paragraph_idx": 14
    }
  ],
